It's usually one of the very first things that doctors want to know about

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.040
if you're not feeling well.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.040
Now, there are a number of things and structures you can see on a chest x-ray,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.440
so let's just go over them real quickly.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.240
Here's a normal chest x-ray.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.600
You can see the lungs.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.880
You can see the heart here in the middle.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.520
You can see the aorta coming off of the heart and supplying blood to the rest of the body.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.480
You can see a number of skeletal structures.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.160
So, for example, here's your collarbone or your clavicle.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.640
You can see all the ribs here on both sides.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.640
You can see the vertebra here in the middle.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.880
This line here is called your diaphragm.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.800
It goes around like that.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.320
And this separates your chest, your thorax, from your abdomen.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.040
Underneath this line is your liver.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.920
Your spleen is over here.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.240
And this little kind of air bubble sitting in here, this is your stomach,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.600
which in this case just has a little bit of air in it.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.680
And again, this is a normal chest x-ray.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.800
Today, we're going to be using a pretty big data set of chest x-rays called the N-ray.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.560
And this is an open source data set of a few thousand chest x-rays,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.520
which happens to have eight different labels.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.640
So, here's the eight different labels available to us in this data set.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.520
And these represent eight pretty commonly seen things on x-rays.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.440
This is not everything that you can see that can go wrong in a chest x-ray,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.720
but this is some of the more common things in the world.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.240
And let's just go through them real quickly.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.760
Here we have atelectasis.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.240
This is when a little piece of the lung kind of deflates a little bit.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.520
So, that is up here.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.080
Basically, this wedge-shaped thing up here in the right upper lobe.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.800
Here we have cardiomegaly.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.960
Cardiomegaly is when you have a really big heart.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:09.840
Specifically, it's when like this length of the heart is more than 50% the length of rib-to-rib.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.360
And this is a sign of heart disease.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.360
Here we have a pleural effusion.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.960
That's when you have something that's basically sitting right outside the lung

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.360
in what's called the pleural space, which is not really supposed to be full of anything.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.040
This one here, this is an infiltrate.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.360
And that's when you have something sitting inside the alveoli of the lungs.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.640
It's not supposed to be there.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.560
There's a few different things that can do that,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.120
but most of the time this means that you have a pneumonia.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.720
In this x-ray, we have a mass.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:52.320
It's this rounded density up here in the left upper lobe apex or on top of the lung.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.320
A mass is something that's more than three centimeters in diameter.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.600
And with the mass, you know, again, it depends on the context,

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.160
but generally we're sort of worried here about some sort of tumor.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.040
This is a nodule, and a nodule is a mass that's smaller than three centimeters.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.480
So this is a little bit harder to see.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:14.560
Here's pneumonia.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.720
A pneumonia is an infection inside of your alveoli.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.920
Again, this is not like mutually exclusive with infiltrate.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.440
And then finally, last but not least, this is a pneumothorax.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.160
And you know how in this one we said you can have sometimes some fluid sitting in the plural space.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.880
Well, here you have air in the plural space.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.680
And this is also known as a collapsed lung.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.040
I bring all this up because some of these conditions are a little bit easier to see

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.720
and some are a little bit harder to see.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.560
For example, you can bet that you can have some of these conditions

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.520
For example, you can bet that a mass, which again is more than three centimeters,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.920
you can bet that that will be easier for us to see than a nodule.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.320
